 ### How is the posted pay read?
 
-From the posting itself, or from the careers site's own pay field where there is one. Only base pay counts: a range that includes bonus, commission or equity is shown as such or left out. When a posting gives a range per level (Microsoft's "IC4" and "IC5", NVIDIA's "Level 4" and "Level 5", the company's own level names), the drawer lists each level's range under **Pay by level**, the card says "· 2 levels", and the headline range runs from the lowest to the highest. When it gives a range for each place (Amazon's pay table, Apple's and Microsoft's ranges for some areas), the drawer lists each place's own range under **Pay by place** and the card says "· N places". A posting that states only a minimum shows it as "from €46K"; an hourly rate that includes the highest shift differential says so ("Incl. top shift pay"), with the stated base beside it when the posting gives one; where a posting states a yearly figure (Austria's minimum, paid in 14 instalments) that figure is used, and the drawer says "Stated as a yearly figure" and quotes the posting. An internship's pay per hour, week or month is shown as posted, also when the posting doesn't say how long the internship lasts; it is never turned into a yearly salary, and a converted figure ("≈ $6,025 a month") is converted at the same interval. The headline median (the stats line over the chart, and Insights' median and middle half) compares one country's pay, as the chart's rows do: when the roles in view are paid in more than one currency, it is the median of the main one ("base pay median (US)"), and the rest are counted, not pooled. The median is written in the currency of the pay it is of, as the cards show pay: Amazon's interns in Vancouver read "CA$101K", Google's Warsaw roles "PLN …", never a dollar figure converted from them; the map's pins and Insights' tiles say the same figure. Part-time hourly pay is also shown as a full-time year of 2,080 hours, for comparison only; when every role in view is paid that way, the median is the hourly rate as posted ("$28.41/hour posted pay median"), and when only some are it says "a year if full-time". Full-time pay posted by the hour is shown at its hourly rate too ("$31.39–54.90/hour"), with its yearly figure at 2,080 hours beside it, marked as converted; sorting, the pay filter and the chart use the yearly figure. The job page gives a yearly figure exactly as the posting states it ("$119,800–234,700", "€46,400"); the card rounds it ("$120K–235K"). A bonus target the posting states as a percentage is shown as posted ("+ 20% bonus target"); a stated "+ 0% bonus target" is no bonus, and "+ bonus mentioned" means the posting mentions a bonus without a figure. None of these is added to the pay. The pay filter compares each role's posted range with yours: a posted minimum ("from €31,100") states no top, so a minimum pay filter never hides it, and pay stated "up to" one figure is never hidden by a maximum; the filter's note counts every role it hides, and why. You can type the amount in another currency ("PLN 150,000", "£75,000", "360 000 zł"): the filter compares it in US dollars at the same fixed rates, and the chip, the link and a saved search keep it as you typed it; a number with no currency is read in the currency of the pay in view when it is all in one, else in dollars.
